{
  "image_name": "e1b4b5974366c8b15866b4613223472a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the 3ω method for measuring a thin film’s cross‑plane thermal conductivity. Overall, it shows how a known film thickness is used with an electrical heating and sensing scheme to extract the layer’s thermal conductivity.\n\nA narrow metal line on top of the thin film acts as both heater and thermometer. An alternating current at frequency ω is applied, producing Joule heating that oscillates at 2ω. This creates a periodic heat wave that travels downward through the film and into the substrate. Because the heater resistance changes with temperature, the 2ω temperature oscillation causes the resistance to oscillate as well, and by Ohm’s law this generates a voltage component at 3ω. Measuring that 3ω voltage allows the temperature response of the stack to be determined.\n\nThe schematic also emphasizes the thermal geometry: for a heater of width 2b on a film of thickness df over a thicker substrate ds, the temperature drop across the thin film is small and the heat flow through the film is treated as essentially one‑dimensional, directed normal to the surface. Some radiation and convection losses appear above the heater, but the main heat path is into the substrate.",
